  So the plan got no Codex review; I've logged that skip in the review ledger. Both tasks are rated "standard" risk, so
  nothing in the plan relies on that review being skipped.

  [1mMain design choice:[0m the repo already has a vendored component kit ([38;5;153mvendor/kit/[39m, from the "Keel admin template" commit)
  with a page header, filter bar, dropdown, sortable table, status badge and empty state. That covers this page. The
  Services page was written before the kit and builds its HTML by hand. I read your "behave as on the Services page" as
  being about behavior: the same query handling, fallbacks, sort links that keep the filter, and a filter that keeps the
  sort. The Deploys page gets that behavior from the kit components instead of copying Services' markup. One small side
  effect: the dropdown auto-submits through [38;5;153mpublic/kit.js[39m, not an inline [38;5;153monchange[39m.

  [1mTwo tasks:[0m
  1. [1mThe page itself[0m ([38;5;153msrc/pages/deploys.js[39m): 10 tests covering the header, columns, default newest-first order, both
     sorts in both directions, all four chip colors, durations and "running", the filter, the empty states, and the
     fallback for unknown query values.
  2. [1mWiring it up:[0m the [38;5;153m/deploys[39m route, the nav link after Services, a README update, and server tests for the page and
     for its 503. The 503 test covers both a missing file and a malformed [38;5;153mdeploys.json[39m.

  [1mGaps in the spec I filled in[0m (they're listed in the plan; tell me if you'd decide any differently):
  - The default direction is [38;5;153mdesc[39m (newest first). So an unknown [38;5;153mdir[39m, or [38;5;153m?sort=service[39m with no [38;5;153mdir[39m, sorts descending.
    Clicking a column header always sends an explicit direction, so clicks never hit this.
  - With "All environments" and no deploys at all, the page says "No deploys".
  - The snapshot time shows as "Snapshot <timestamp>", like the Overview page.
  - Chip colors use the kit's built-in colors: green, red, amber and blue for succeeded, failed, rolled-back and
    in-progress.
  - Two deploys of the same service stay in snapshot order when sorting by Service. The spec doesn't ask for a
    tie-breaker.

  To check the plan, I ran its code in a throwaway copy of the repo: all 30 tests passed (18 existing plus 12 new). Port
  3123 was already in use on this machine, so I changed the plan's final check to call the request handler directly
  instead of starting the server.

